WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.547
My name is Sarah Perry.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:08.216
I'm the author of the novel The Essex Serpent

00:00:08.216 --> 00:00:10.343
which this series is based on.

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:14.222
I felt very strongly that I wanted to hand it over to somebody that I trusted

00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:17.350
and I have confidence in Anna and in the production company.

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:20.311
She has been incredibly generous in allowing me

00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:23.690
to make her amazing book work for screen.

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:30.113
The most important thing when adapting is to keep the soul of the book.

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:33.116
It's really beautifully written.

00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:35.326
It's a wonderful adaptation of the book.

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:38.163
Anna had retained so much of Sarah's novel

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:40.457
but she'd also added something as well.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:42.292
A lot of interiority.

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:46.004
With a screenplay you don't have the luxury of so much description.

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:50.800
You have to fold the depth into the characters actions as opposed to their words.

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:57.307
There is this feeling of handing something over

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.476
and saying "I trust you completely."

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.686
My first instinct was right 

00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:03.980
that they knew exactly what they needed to do with it.

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:05.774
Should we walk?

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:09.652
It’s so exciting to see your work brought to life on film.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:11.696
Sarah Perry has been very generous

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:15.784
in allowing us to take the series to some different places.

00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:18.661
I am here to be an extra. 

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.957
We are filming a scene at the Natural History Museum

00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:25.835
where Will Ransome unexpectedly meets Cora.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:27.921
This is a scene that isn’t in the book.

00:01:28.213 --> 00:01:30.131
It sort of achieves two things I think. 

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:33.885
It enables Cora and Will to have an additional form of meeting  

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:36.137
but also really importantly it shows Cora 

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:38.890
as somebody who is deeply interested in paleontology.

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:41.184
Very at home in the Natural History Museum.

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:44.270
It’s been really amazing and actually quite uncanny 

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.232
to suddenly be living inside of one of my own imaginary worlds.

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:53.279
What Sarah Perry did so brilliantly in the novel 

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.864
and we have tried to bring to life

00:01:54.864 --> 00:01:57.575
is that all the female characters are so unexpected. 

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.286
Nothing about their behavior is

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:03.706
fitting this stereotype of victorian, subservient

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:05.208
constrained female.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:10.213
Sarah managed to create characters that are completely of their time 

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.090
but also strikingly contemporary. 

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:15.260
You’re furious because I didn’t ask your permission! 

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:18.930
Even now, Cora Seaborne is described as a modern heroine

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.932
and she is absolutely not.

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:23.977
She is as educated and as independent

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:28.064
and as socially aware as 19th century women were at the time.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.109
Well if we were welcomed into professions then we might have vocations too.

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:32.652
I couldn't agree more. 

00:02:34.904 --> 00:02:37.949
The most challenging aspect of any adaptation

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:43.788
 is just trying to achieve a series which people who loved the book will love as much.

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.332
But also, for people who haven’t read the book 

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.334
to fall in love with it in its own right.

00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:51.838
The demand I suppose was for us to be brave enough 

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.381
to meet Sarah where she started.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.885
One’s imagination can take all these different shapes and forms

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:59.721
and live on beyond even what I had imagined it would do.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:01.264
It has been a joy to see.

